    {
      "id": "ed7d66d7-0c21-4f1a-aebf-7017a522a5a3",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -3488,
        -608
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 896,
        "height": 656,
        "content": "# Email triage with decision model Jev, Telegram and Google Sheets\n\n### How it works\n\n1. The workflow watches Gmail for new incoming messages and fetches each full email.\n2. It formats the email into a request for Jev, sends it to the OpenRouter decision API, and normalizes the response.\n3. It maps the classification fields into Google Sheets columns and appends a tracking row.\n4. It checks whether the email is urgent and, if so, sends a Telegram alert.\n5. It applies a Gmail project label to keep the inbox organized.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- [ ] Connect Gmail credentials for both the trigger and the follow-up Gmail action.\n- [ ] Configure the Jev/OpenRouter HTTP request with the correct endpoint, auth header, and request body format.\n- [ ] Review the code nodes to ensure the email payload and Jev response fields match your data structure.\n- [ ] Connect Google Sheets credentials and select the target spreadsheet and worksheet.\n- [ ] Map the sheet columns in the column-selection code node to match your tracking schema.\n- [ ] Set up Telegram credentials and choose the destination chat or channel for urgent alerts.\n- [ ] Confirm the Gmail label name used by the label action exists or will be created as needed.\n\n### Customization\nYou can change the urgency rule in the IF node, adjust which email fields are sent to Jev, and expand the Google Sheets row schema to track additional metadata."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
